    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe audit emission sits in the right unit. Creation-time concerns (capacity, household uniqueness, daily limit, customer code, namesake count) already live together in `saveOwner`, so emitting the create audit line here is consistent with how the method is organized rather than a foreign responsibility.\n\n- Capturing `boolean isCreate = owner.isNew()` at the top is necessary and correct: `save()` assigns the id, flipping `isNew()` to false, so the flag must be read before persistence and the log read after (to capture the generated id). This is the natural way to express it.\n- The dedicated `private static final Logger AUDIT` with the `\"AUDIT\"` name matches the spec and the conventional SLF4J idiom used throughout Spring code; the Javadoc explaining its purpose is a nice touch and not excessive.\n- No duplication introduced, and the class isn't becoming a catch-all \u2014 this is squarely owner-persistence logic in the owner-persistence service.\n- Logging after `ownerRepository.save(owner)` correctly reflects \"on successful create,\" since a failed save would throw before reaching the log.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find this placement and style natural.",
